WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.769
Welcome to Horizon
installer training, Part 3.

2
00:00:05.207 --> 00:00:07.024
Wire, parts and water.

3
00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.462
By the end, you will know
which wire to use where,

4
00:00:10.462 --> 00:00:13.820
how to keep water out, and
why long runs lose volts.

5
00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.910
You will also know what has to be
in the truck before you go to a job.

6
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.621
Start with the weakest wire in
the system. The light string.

7
00:00:23.071 --> 00:00:27.222
Our lights come with 20 gauge wire
built in. We cannot change that.

8
00:00:27.672 --> 00:00:29.965
Everything we run up to it has one job.

9
00:00:30.415 --> 00:00:34.090
Make sure that 20 gauge wire
never sees more than 4 amps.

10
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.739
Everything upstream of the light
string is there to protect it.

11
00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:41.236
Here is the first thing
to know about wire.

12
00:00:41.236 --> 00:00:43.484
Thicker wire has a smaller gauge number.

13
00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:46.146
14 gauge is the thickest. It works,

14
00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:49.304
but it is more than we
need. Use up the stock.

15
00:00:49.754 --> 00:00:54.404
16 gauge carries 4 amps easily.
This is our feeder wire. Use this.

16
00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:58.506
18 gauge is also fine for a 4 amp circuit.

17
00:00:58.956 --> 00:01:01.525
20 gauge is the light string. It is thin,

18
00:01:01.525 --> 00:01:04.407
it is built in, and it is what we protect.

19
00:01:05.757 --> 00:01:07.935
Now, which wire goes where.

20
00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:15.171
From the box to an injection, the feeder
is 16/2 or 18/2. FT-4, and stranded.

21
00:01:15.621 --> 00:01:20.074
It carries 4 amps with room to spare,
and it is light and easy to pull.

22
00:01:20.524 --> 00:01:24.048
14/2 is allowed. Use
up what is in the shop.

23
00:01:24.498 --> 00:01:28.974
But thicker does not make it safer. The
20 gauge light wire is still the limit.

24
00:01:29.424 --> 00:01:32.815
Going through a wall,
use FT-4 with an ETL,

25
00:01:32.815 --> 00:01:36.141
CSA or UL number printed on the jacket.

26
00:01:36.591 --> 00:01:40.727
Check every spool. No printed number,
and it does not go through a wall.

27
00:01:40.727 --> 00:01:43.447
No exceptions. Ask Ben
if you are not sure.

28
00:01:43.897 --> 00:01:47.023
And always use stranded
copper. Never solid core.

29
00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:50.590
Solid copper hardens, and
snaps at the terminations.

30
00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.874
Next, keeping water out.

31
00:01:54.324 --> 00:01:56.708
Put dielectric grease in every connector.

32
00:01:56.708 --> 00:02:00.564
A squirt before you push it
together. It pushes the water out.

33
00:02:01.014 --> 00:02:03.566
The little O-ring is what seals a light.

34
00:02:03.566 --> 00:02:06.559
Not the plastic body.
So grease the O-ring.

35
00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:10.551
Inside-only connectors, the
lever nuts, are not waterproof.

36
00:02:10.551 --> 00:02:14.283
They go inside the box only.
Never outside in the weather.

37
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:16.655
And do not over-tighten the barrel.

38
00:02:16.655 --> 00:02:19.650
Tighter is not a better
seal. The O-ring seals it.

39
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.299
Over-tightening just makes the
light hard to change later.

40
00:02:24.649 --> 00:02:28.521
Here is a rule for every job.
Never plug in with the power on.

41
00:02:28.971 --> 00:02:32.112
Shut the power off at the
box first. Every time.

42
00:02:32.562 --> 00:02:33.833
On a run over 30 lights,

43
00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:37.897
plugging in live kills the chip in
the light before the one you touched.

44
00:02:38.347 --> 00:02:42.394
You will not see it go. It
comes back later as a callout.

45
00:02:42.844 --> 00:02:47.296
So it is always the same order.
Power off. Plug in. Power on. Test.

46
00:02:48.646 --> 00:02:51.021
Now, long runs lose volts.

47
00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:55.796
Wire is not perfect. The further
power travels, the more it drops.

48
00:02:56.246 --> 00:03:02.870
We tested 50 feet of 20 gauge wire.
In at 24.1 volts. Out at 22.7.

49
00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:07.610
That is a 5.8% drop.
Just past our 5% limit.

50
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.340
On the job, if the far-end
lights go dim or yellow,

51
00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.034
that is voltage drop. Not a bad light.

52
00:03:14.484 --> 00:03:18.484
The fix is never a bigger
supply. It is a closer injection.

53
00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:22.864
Under 22.8 volts at the last
light? Move the injection.

54
00:03:24.214 --> 00:03:26.368
Last, what lives in the truck.

55
00:03:26.818 --> 00:03:30.493
A clamp meter for amps,
and a multimeter for volts.

56
00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:36.139
Dielectric grease, 16/2 FT-4
wire, and spare 4 amp breakers.

57
00:03:36.589 --> 00:03:39.001
Lever nuts, for inside the box only.

58
00:03:39.001 --> 00:03:43.004
The marked-up job photo, and
the test sheet from Part 6.

59
00:03:43.257 --> 00:03:45.852
And remember. No meter, no install.

60
00:03:47.202 --> 00:03:48.323
Let's lock it in.

61
00:03:48.773 --> 00:03:51.634
The 20 gauge light string
is the weakest wire.

62
00:03:51.634 --> 00:03:54.061
It must never see more than 4 amps.

63
00:03:54.511 --> 00:04:01.902
The feeder is 16/2 or 18/2, FT-4,
stranded. Use up 14/2, but it is not safer.

64
00:04:02.352 --> 00:04:05.339
Through a wall, only FT-4 with an ETL,

65
00:04:05.339 --> 00:04:09.440
CSA or UL number on the
jacket. Never solid core.

66
00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.920
Grease every connector and O-ring.

67
00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.573
Lever nuts stay in the box.
Power off before you plug in.

68
00:04:16.023 --> 00:04:18.530
Under 22.8 volts at the last light,

69
00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:22.218
move the injection closer.
No meter, no install.

70
00:04:22.668 --> 00:04:27.700
Next, take the Part 3 quiz. Then
in Part 4, we learn how to wire it.
